## 0. Why no golden moved, and why that is not evidence the fix is inert

The golden rule at plan L1630 says C6e's goldens are *expected* to move. None did.
That needed an explanation before I would sign the commit, so I proved one rather
than assuming it.

`fairShareValue` for a model is `Σ_role claim_role`, and `allocateForModel`'s budget
is `target = w.remaining − allocationMean`. `fairShareScaleUp` sets
`allocationMean = 0` whenever `len(active) == 1`. So **a single-model fixture gets
`target == claim`**: the entitlement is exactly the model's whole claim and can
never bind, no matter how the roles draw against it. Every existing golden is
single-model-per-pass and demand-bound with a generous pool. That is precisely why
the double-spend was invisible in them — not because the fix does nothing.

I demonstrated the delta empirically instead. `git show HEAD:...greedy_score_optimizer.go`
into `/tmp`, swapped the pre-C6e file in, ran the new Context, restored. On the old
code the new fixture gives `prefill-v = 7` (6 GPUs) **and** `decode-v = 4` (6 GPUs)
= **12 GPUs out of a 7-GPU pool**, driving `available` to −5. On the new code it is
`2` and `4` = exactly **7 GPUs**. Both W1 specs fail on old code and pass on new;
the determinism spec passes on both. The counterfactual is recorded in the test
comment so the next reader does not have to redo it.

Two consequences for you:

- **The plan's "the pool was enforced, the fair share was not" is only half true in
  the paired path.** The pool check does bound the single-role path, but in
  `allocateForModelPaired` the old code drove the pool *negative*, so it was not
  enforced there either. That is item 2 below. I wrote the dev-guide paragraph to
  say the doubled draw "surfaced as a fair-share violation rather than as a
  failure" and did not claim the pool was airtight.
- If you want a golden to move for C6e, it needs a **two-model** fixture, since the
  entitlement only binds under contention. I did not add one — the three new specs
  in the `Fair Share — One Entitlement per Model` Context already construct
  contention via a second "mean-setter" model, and they are unit specs rather than
  goldens. Say the word if you want the same scenario promoted to a golden.

